  {
    "title": "A vowel before a double consonant, ck or tz is short",
    "body": "Two consonant letters after a vowel show that the vowel is short and crisp. The consonant itself is said only once, not held: ck is just “k” and tz is “ts”.",
    "pattern": "[aeiouäöü](?=bb|dd|ff|gg|ll|mm|nn|pp|rr|ss|tt|ck|tz)",
    "examples": [
      {
        "word": "kommen",
        "say": "KOM-uhn",
        "en": "to come"
      },
      {
        "word": "essen",
        "say": "ES-uhn",
        "en": "to eat"
      },
      {
        "word": "die Katze",
        "say": "dee KAT-suh",
        "en": "cat"
      },
      {
        "word": "das Bett",
        "say": "dass bet",
        "en": "bed"
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "title": "o is a pure “oh” when long, short like “hot”",
    "body": "Long o is a pure “oh” with rounded lips, without the “w” glide of English “go”. Short o is like the “o” in British “hot”.",
    "pattern": "(?<!o)o(?![ou])",
    "examples": [
