      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "title": "Final e is never silent: say “uh”",
    "body": "An e at the end of a word is always pronounced, as a short weak “uh” like the “a” in “sofa”. Only ee (Tee, Idee) and ie (die, Sie) are different.",
    "pattern": "(?<=\\p{L})(?<![ie])e(?!\\p{L})",
    "examples": [
      {
        "word": "ich habe",
        "say": "ikh HAH-buh",
        "en": "I have"
      },
      {
        "word": "bitte",
        "say": "BIT-uh",
        "en": "please"
      },
      {
        "word": "die Tasche",
        "say": "dee TASH-uh",
        "en": "bag"
      },
      {
        "word": "alle",
        "say": "AL-uh",
        "en": "all"
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "title": "r before a vowel is made in the throat",
    "body": "Before a vowel, r is a light gargled sound at the back of the throat, close to a soft French r. A rolled tongue-tip r, common in Bavaria and Austria, is also correct. Never use the English r.",
    "pattern": "r(?=[aeiouäöüy])",
    "examples": [
