  {
    "title": "b, d, g at the end of a syllable sound like p, t, k",
    "body": "At the end of a word or syllable, and before most consonants, b, d and g lose their buzz and become “p”, “t”, “k”. dt is also a plain “t”: Stadt.",
    "pattern": "(?:b|d|(?<!n|(?<!e)i)g)(?![aeiouäöüylrj])",
